<main class="container doc-page" style="max-width:820px">

    <h1>About SocialNet</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
        A social network built as a final project for the Web Application
        Development course.
    </p>

    <%# §32.iii — multiple-columns. Long-form text is where newspaper columns
        genuinely help; column-width lets the browser drop to one column on a
        narrow screen without a media query. %>
    <div class="prose-columns">

        <p>
            SocialNet lets people post text, images and video, form groups around
            shared interests, connect with friends, and see a feed drawn from both.
            Everything you see was written by our team.
        </p>

        <h3>How it is built</h3>
        <p>
            The server runs on Node.js with Express, following the
            Model&ndash;View&ndash;Controller pattern. Views are rendered with EJS.
            Data lives in MongoDB, accessed through the official native driver
            rather than an ORM.
        </p>
        <p>
            Document shapes are enforced by MongoDB's own <code>$jsonSchema</code>
            validators, so a malformed record is rejected by the database itself
            rather than relying on application code remembering to check.
        </p>

        <h3>Security</h3>
        <p>
            Passwords are never stored. They are hashed with bcrypt, which is
            deliberately slow and salts every password individually, so two people
            choosing the same password still produce different hashes.
        </p>
        <p>
            Sessions hold only an identifier and a display name. Access rules are
            enforced on the server: hiding a button in the interface is a courtesy,
            not a security measure, so every restricted action is checked again
            before it runs.
        </p>

        <h3>Live data</h3>
        <p>
            The statistics pages are drawn with D3.js from aggregation queries that
            run against the database when you load the page &mdash; nothing is
            precomputed or hard-coded.
        </p>
        <p>
            Weather is fetched from Open-Meteo by our own server, interpreted, and
            rendered with our markup. The service returns numeric weather codes; the
            words and icons you see are produced by us.
        </p>

        <h3>Interface</h3>
        <p>
            Search updates as you type using asynchronous requests, with input
            debounced so a keystroke is not a database query, and superseded
            requests cancelled so results never arrive out of order.
        </p>
        <p>
            The stylesheet is written from scratch &mdash; no framework, no template.
            Type is set in Outfit, a variable typeface under the SIL Open Font
            Licence, served from our own server so the site looks right without an
            internet connection.
        </p>

    </div>

</main>

